Let $F$ be a field of positive characteristic $p>2$.

Suppose $A\subset F$, $|A|\leq p^{2/3}$. Let $P=A\times A$.

Consider the equation
\begin{equation}\label{engy}
 \frac{a-c}{b-c} = \frac{a'-c'}{b'-c'}:\, a,b,c,a',b',c'\in A.
 \end{equation}
 Rewrite it as
\begin{equation}\label{engy1}
(a-c)b' - ac' - (a'-c')b + ca' = 0.
\end{equation}
 Note that this is the same as
 \begin{equation}\label{det} \det \left( \begin{array}{ccc}1&1&1\\a&b&c\\a'&b'&c'\end{array}\right)=0.
 \end{equation}
 A solution represents an incidence of a point in $FP^3$, with homogeneous coordinates
 $(a-c: a: b: c)$ and a plane with dual homogeneous coordinates  $(b': -c': c'-a' : a')$. The number of the above quadruples of homogeneous coordinates is clearly $|A|^3$ each. Moreover, we can assume without loss of generality that each such quadruple of homogeneous coordinates defines a different point/plane in $FP^3$.

 Indeed, the expression \eqref{engy} is invariant to any shift $A\rightarrow A+ \xi$, where $\xi$ can be chosen in the algebraic closure of $F$. Taking any  $\xi\not\in \frac{AA-AA}{A+A-A-A}$ ensures that without loss of generality one may assume that each ratio $a/b$  appears only once in $A$. This suffices.
